# What does NOT go into the published skill bundle.
#
# The rule this file follows: everything needed to run the skill, and to read
# it, ships; everything that only exists to develop it does not.
#
# **src/ ships. The test suite does not.** The non-test source ships because the
# auditability claim is the product: README.md, PRIVACY.md and SECURITY.md all
# invite a reader to check the guarantees in the code, and shipping src/ next to
# the lib/ built from it is what makes those two comparable.
#
# The tests used to ship alongside it, to be read rather than run. That was
# reversed on 2026-08-19, for two reasons that only became visible once the
# bundle was scanned.
#
# The first is that they were never runnable here anyway. An installed copy has
# no vitest.config.ts, no scripts/ and no .github/, all excluded below, and two
# of the tests read paths from those directories (src/docs.test.ts reads
# .github/CODEOWNERS, src/cli/shim.test.ts reads scripts/build-lib.mjs). So the
# bundle carried 25 files that could only ever be read, never executed.
#
# The second is what a scanner reads them as. ClawHub's static analysis reported
# four CRITICAL findings against the 0.1.0 bundle and every one was a test file:
# child_process calls in src/cli/shim.test.ts and src/release-workflow.test.ts,
# and the deliberately fake credential fixtures in src/auth/credentials.test.ts
# and src/privacy/redact.test.ts. Those fixtures are load-bearing (a test that
# proves a bearer token gets redacted has to contain a bearer token to redact)
# and the child_process calls test this repository's own machinery, not the
# skill. All four are false positives, and all four were being reported against
# the artifact a user installs.
#
# What replaces them: the tests live in the public repository, where they can be
# run rather than only read. Every doc that cites one as evidence now says so and
# links there. That is a better offer than an unrunnable copy in the bundle.
#
# lib/ ships because it is what actually runs.
#
# Verify a change here with:
#   clawhub skill publish "$PWD" --slug open-ga4 --name "Open GA4" \
#     --version 0.2.0 --dry-run --json
# and read fileCount. --dry-run publishes nothing.

# Dependencies and version control. Named explicitly rather than assumed:
# .github/workflows/release.yml publishes after `npm ci`, so node_modules is
# present in the directory being published, and whether the publisher happens
# to skip it is not something this repository controls or tests. There are two
# such directories during a release, the project's own and the publisher's
# pinned install under .github/publish/; this pattern has no leading slash, so
# it matches both at any depth, and .github/ below excludes the second anyway.
node_modules/
.git/
package-lock.json

# Credentials. These patterns mirror .gitignore's own credential block
# deliberately, and src/build.test.ts asserts the two files still agree, so a
# pattern added to one and forgotten in the other fails the suite.
#
# Why this file needs them at all, given .gitignore has them. .gitignore
# decides what reaches git; this file decides what leaves the machine, and the
# two are not the same publish path. The real release publishes a clean CI
# checkout, where no such file exists, so nothing here is exposed today. The
# local form documented at the top of this file is the one that matters:
# `clawhub skill publish "$PWD"` uploads the working tree it is pointed at, and
# a working tree is exactly where a key file lands while somebody is setting
# this up or reproducing a bug. Git would refuse to commit it and the registry
# would publish it. That asymmetry is the whole reason for this block.
#
# Named rather than assumed, the same rule the rest of this file follows: no
# claim about what the publisher happens to skip is being made here.
#
# One line of .gitignore's block is deliberately not mirrored: `!.env.example`,
# the negation that lets an example file be committed. No such file exists, and
# an ignore rule for a file that is not there protects nothing while looking
# like protection. If one is ever added and is worth shipping, add the
# negation then, in this block, where the reasoning is.
*.pem
*.key
*-key.json
*service-account*.json
application_default_credentials.json
.env
.env.*

# Build and test configuration. The skill runs from lib/, which is committed;
# nothing in the installed folder compiles or runs a test suite.
tsconfig.json
tsconfig.check.json
vitest.config.ts
scripts/

# The test suite itself, and the helpers that exist only to support it. See the
# header for why these stopped shipping. src/testing/ holds no runtime code:
# every file in it is imported by a test and by nothing else.
*.test.ts
src/testing/

# Repository automation and local working state. CI, Dependabot and the
# release workflow describe how this repository is maintained, not what the
# skill does. .superpowers/ and .sandbox/ are working directories.
.github/
.superpowers/
.sandbox/

# The plans and specs for building the skill, as opposed to the design record
# for the skill itself. docs/DESIGN.md ships and these do not, the same split
# src/testing/files.test-support.ts already makes for the documentation tests:
# these describe what was rejected and what changed on the way here, which is
# repository history rather than anything a user or a reviewer of the skill
# needs.
docs/superpowers/
